  "judge": {
    "dimensions": [
      {
        "name": "correctness",
        "score": 2,
        "rationale": "The rubric asks for the slow work to move into its own job that carries the timeout, with `test` turned into a short gate job using needs: and if: always() that fails unless the work job succeeded. That way the required context is never reported as cancelled. The agent only raised timeout-minutes from 10 to 30. A slow enough run can still hit the limit, and the required `test` check would still be reported as cancelled and block the merge. The agent correctly saw that the timeout caused the cancellation, but the fix doesn't address the structural problem."
      },
      {
        "name": "explanation",
        "score": 5,
        "rationale": "The agent said GitHub cancelled the job when it went past the limit and that a cancelled required check blocks merging. This partly covers the point that GitHub reports a timeout as cancelled. It did not explain that this cancelled status is distinct from failure, or why a gate job with if: always() avoids it. The note about the workflow sitting outside .github/workflows/ is a useful observation."
      },
      {
        "name": "restraint",
        "score": 9,
        "rationale": "The diff changes one line with no unrelated edits. The agent mentioned sharding without making changes and was honest that it had not run or pushed anything."
      }
    ],
    "overall": 3.5
  },
